[Music]  hello and welcome to the session  for your own good with me marianne c  cart and  a great panel who i will introduce in a  minute  now we're all familiar with the  hippocratic famous line do no harm  fewer know perhaps the more important  line above all  i must not play it god but with life  spans increasing often long past the  point of comfort  it's not unusual for treatments to cause  more suffering mental as well as  physical  than the disease itself so some argue  that doctors have forgotten the promises  they made at medical school  should they recognize that they are  sacrificing well-being  in the name of longevity and pledge to  return to the original values of do no  harm  or is this a dangerous and misguided  strategy that threatens the life and  well-being of us all  well here to debate this are henry marsh  who is one of the uk's leading  neurosurgeons  and author of the best-selling memoir  called do no harm  sarah wooten is the chief executive of  dignity and dying  and co-author of a new book called last  rites  ghts on the subject of care at the end  of life  and natasha vita moore is an author  strategic designer and scientist  working in the fields of human  enhancement technology and life  extension  so as usual i'm going to ask each of  them to do a three-minute pitch to us  all  uh on the subject are doctors  sacrificing well-being  for the sake of longevity and first of  all sarah sarah wooten  imagine having to say goodbye to your  dying daughter seven times  fiona strong was 31 and she had a rare  cancer that had started in her sinuses  and invaded her brain and her nose  by 2018 it was also in her neck shoulder  and spine the tumor was growing and  fiona was finding it difficult to  swallow  and to breathe so the hospice offered to  sedate her  so she wouldn't be aware of her symptoms  when she died  but instead of dying peacefully in her  sleep fiona woke up  just 20 minutes later confused  scared and in pain  she underwent five days of continual  waking and pain before she died  each time her mother would say another  last goodbye to her daughter  but it was only after seven farewells  that fiona eventually died  the blanket ban on assisted dying denied  fiona the option of shortening what  anybody would agree  was a horrendous death and i argue that  nowhere  is the tension between doctor knows best  paternalism  and patient choice more evident and  actually more harmful  than at the end of life doctors are  undoubtedly undoubtedly sacrificing the  well-being  of dying people but not necessarily for  the sake of longevity  fiona's care team absolutely recognized  that her needlessly drawn out death  was not in anybody's best interests the  hospice nurses were crying too  throughout those five days it was  because of a lack of better options  and clinging to the old ways of doing  things  i believe that medical practice should  react to the attitudes and values of  society rather than waiting for the  notoriously conservative medical  profession  to evolve remember doctors objected to  the nhs  they objected to the contraceptive pill  and they objected to the  decriminalization of abortion  some doctors even objected to providing  pain relief in  childbirth in last rites  we argue that there are parallels  between the campaign for assisted dying  and other progressive social campaigns  like votes for women like racial justice  and equal marriage  people have had to fight against a  stubborn stubborn establishment which  refuses to listen  that's not how it has to be 150 million  people have access to your sister dying  now  across the u.s australia and canada and  soon to be new zealand probably  over 80 percent of people in the uk want  the law to change here  it is proven to work the recent  cumberland  report on medical devices and their  safety is a sad reminder  of how women have been harmed by a  medical culture that's been all too  willing  to ignore belittle and gaslight them  any similar review of choice at the end  of life in the future will find that  this country has persisted with a  blanket ban on sister dying for  far too long and that a broken status  quo was propped up by an establishment  which doctors yes are part of that  willingly allowed a vocal minority to  ignore evidence  and override the expressed will of dying  people we shouldn't have to wait for  such a review to tell us what we know  now  not when the status quo inflicts so much  harm on people  like fiona and their families  sarah thank you very much henry marsh  you're part of the medical establishment  what's your pitch  um my book was called do no harm because  of course it's an ironical  title because the practice of medicine  of modern medicine  inevitably involves harm either the risk  of harm  and treatment failing or harm which is  needed to get a good result  and the decision-making is is very  difficult  um it's not as simple  a question of adding up the equations  and saying well if we do this  there's a chance of that happening and  if we don't etc etc  instead you have to balance the risks of  treatment  against the possible benefits and often  we don't know this is about  probabilities there is clear clearly  particularly in america  there is excessive treatment going on  particularly chemotherapy  for cancer we know that in america  now a very very significant part of  people's lifetime  medical expenses are spent in the last  few months of their life in other words  it's wasted but it's not this is not  just  because of doctors being inhuman  insensitive  it is also because as human beings we  are very reluctant to  stop hooping and if you have a drug  where there's a  five percent chance it will prolong your  life  um we all will hope and think we'll be  one of the five percent lucky ones  and forget the 95 chance of failure and  miserable  side effects so it's really a sort of  foliar dirt  between doctors and patients often which  results in  so much over treatment because life  without hope  is very difficult a very good friend of  mine has just been diagnosed with a  highly malignant  brain tumor and i wasn't involved in his  treatment  and because it's my own specialty i  inevitably got involved with him and his  family  and i he's going to have treatment he's  my age  in fact the statistics are the value of  radiotherapy and a man his age with a  height of an english inoperable brain  tumor  yes it really is nothing probably  and yet it's very difficult to say no i  mean the  doctors treating him have recommended it  he has gone wrong with that  um and i face the problem should i tell  him well in my opinion  it's not worth it what would i say if it  was me  and of course that again brings me back  to the fact there's a great quote from  the hospital that neither the deaf  nor son can be looked at steadily we do  find it very very difficult  to think about our own death i had to  tell my friend you've only got a few  months left to live  the next few weeks are the best few  weeks left in your life  of that there's no doubt um  and yet it's so difficult having said  that though i do feel very strongly that  the i suspect a minority of  many palliative care doctors in this  country was so  opposed to assisted dying i regarded as  highly patronizing the sister dying  is not about licensing doctors to kill  people is about patient autonomy and  about allowing us as patients to decide  how we want to end our lives having said  that it is very difficult  and we know from the experience in  oregon that people with a terminal  diagnosis  of six months express an interest in  assisted dying when they go into  palliative care  but often don't take up that option  towards the end  and i know several families medical  families  colleagues of mine whose parents were  doctors who said to their children  you know don't i don't want to die like  this i'd  help me bring it to an end but when the  end did come in fact they wouldn't talk  about it and they developed a sort of  split consciousness many of us do  develop when we're dying  part of us thinks we're going to go and  living forever part of us means we're  dying  so it is it's very difficult but there's  no question about the law in this  country is wrong  and should be changed okay  natasha vitamin you're in america what  do you think  thank you marianne lovely to be here i'd  like to make a couple of basic points  uh the dictum do no harm is a latin  phrase that stems from the  the greek hypocritical oath but we have  to remember that that oath was written  in the fifth century  during a time when very little was known  about biology  genetics and the human genome so we not  only did not see into the body into  genomics into proteinomics into all the  different layers  of the worker bees inside the body  continually working  in a symphony to keep the body healthy  and alive  to continue watching this video click  the link in the top left or in the  description below  or visit iai dot tv for more debates and  talks from the world's leading thinkers  on today's biggest ideas

